# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data. ~52 GB: the four cohort TSVs plus the pre-built AGORA2/AGORA103 community
# models (~36 GB) and MICOM solver artefacts. Source cohorts are cited by
# accession in the manuscript and the model build is documented, not shipped.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Data/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated outputs. Every figure and table is reproducible from a pipeline run
# against a tagged commit; the frozen baseline is archived for deposition
# separately. Notebooks recreate this tree via mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Results/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
env/

# joblib / Optuna caches written by ml_workbench
.joblib_cache/
*.optuna-journal*

# Jupyter
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Local tooling state (personal, not shared)
.claude/settings.local.json

# Dardel deployment config: host, username, allocation id and absolute paths.
# Personal and site-specific; dardel.env.example is the tracked template.
v2/dardel/dardel.env

# Two kinds of litter the Dardel checkout accumulates: the transfer scratch
# 02_stage_inputs.sh leaves behind, and the SLURM job logs, which land next to the
# batch script because that is the submission directory. Both have to be ignored
# rather than deleted: run_pipeline.py reads `git status --porcelain`, which counts
# untracked files, so either one makes every run on that machine dirty -- and a dirty
# run keeps Results/SMOKE_RUN.txt and can never become a baseline. The job logs are
# also the only copy of a run's stdout, so deleting them to get a clean tree would
# throw away the record. They cannot move into Results/logs/ either: archive_results
# relocates that tree mid-run, underneath the job still writing to it.
.stage/
v2/dardel/slurm-*.out

# The TrEMBL arm of the enzyme probe: 19 MB of sequence that is fully determined by
# members_trembl.tsv, which IS committed and pins every accession. Rebuild it with
# `python v2/reads/build_enzyme_db.py --unreviewed`. The Swiss-Prot arm is small and
# is committed as sequence, because it is the arm that supports a presence claim.
v2/reads/enzymes/enzymes_trembl.faa

# The full UniRef90 row-name index: 42 MB, all 8,930,907 row names of
# gene_families.rda. Kept on disk because having it means no later question needs a
# second five-minute load of the 3.26 GB object, but it is a mechanical dump of the
# source matrix and extract_gene_families.sbatch regenerates it byte for byte. The
# small evidence tables in the same directory ARE committed, because the chapter cites
# their numbers and a reader should not need Dardel access to check them.
v2/genes/out/rownames_index.txt.gz

# The background set's raw abundances: 4.4 MB of (row, sample, abundance) triples for
# 6,335 non-target gene families. Same reasoning as the row-name index -- it is an
# input to a control, not a result, and background.R regenerates it exactly (the draw
# is seeded, SEED = 20261108, and background_matching.tsv records the decile-by-decile
# counts it produced). What the chapter cites is background_trend.tsv, which IS
# committed at 1 MB along with the matching table and provenance.
v2/genes/out/gf_background_long.tsv.gz

# The community models and solved pickles `diet_screen.py --community N` builds to
# compare the two media on real samples. Hundreds of MB of micom community models plus
# their GrowthResults, rebuilt by re-running the flag. The comparison itself --
# out/community_check.tsv -- is small and IS committed.
v2/micom/out/community/

# OS and Office cruft
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
~$*

# surgical-edit backups (git is the backup)
*.bak
